{
  "id": "python-601",
  "name": "Python 601 · Data Science, Metrics & Reporting",
  "description": "Analyze data and build reports using Arima's built-in rendering — barista.table, barista.stats, HTML metric cards, and inline SVG charts (no install) — then scale up with NumPy, pandas, and matplotlib from PyPI.",
  "metadata": {
    "category": "tutorial",
    "language": "python",
    "level": 601,
    "subcategory": "Data Science & Analytics"
  },
  "cells": [
    {
      "id": "py601-md0",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "source": "# Python 601 · Data Science, Metrics & Reporting\n\nTurn raw data into insight and **rendered reports** — right inside a notebook cell.\n\nArima gives every Python cell built-in rendering helpers, so you can build metrics and dashboards **with zero dependencies**:\n- `barista.table(rows)` — tabular reports\n- `barista.stats(values)` — instant summary statistics\n- `barista.html(markup)` — render KPI cards, badges, **inline SVG charts**\n- `barista.image(fig)` — render a matplotlib figure as an image\n\nWe start with pure-Python analysis + rendering (always runs), then scale up with **NumPy / pandas / matplotlib** from the **PyPI** tab.\n\n**Prerequisites:** *Python 301*.",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "py601-md1",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "source": "## 1. A dataset + summary statistics\n\nHere's a month of sales rows. `barista.stats()` gives count / min / max / mean / std for any list of numbers.",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "py601-1",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "python",
      "source": "//@ anchor: sales-data\nsales = [\n    {\"region\": \"North\", \"product\": \"Widget\", \"units\": 120, \"revenue\": 3600},\n    {\"region\": \"North\", \"product\": \"Gadget\", \"units\": 80,  \"revenue\": 4000},\n    {\"region\": \"South\", \"product\": \"Widget\", \"units\": 200, \"revenue\": 6000},\n    {\"region\": \"South\", \"product\": \"Gadget\", \"units\": 60,  \"revenue\": 3000},\n    {\"region\": \"East\",  \"product\": \"Widget\", \"units\": 150, \"revenue\": 4500},\n    {\"region\": \"East\",  \"product\": \"Gadget\", \"units\": 110, \"revenue\": 5500},\n    {\"region\": \"West\",  \"product\": \"Widget\", \"units\": 90,  \"revenue\": 2700},\n    {\"region\": \"West\",  \"product\": \"Gadget\", \"units\": 130, \"revenue\": 6500},\n]\nprint(f\"{len(sales)} rows loaded\")\nprint(\"Revenue summary:\")\nbarista.stats([r[\"revenue\"] for r in sales])",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0,
      "anchor": "sales-data"
    },
    {
      "id": "py601-md2",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "source": "## 2. Aggregate + tabular report\n\nGroup by region in pure Python and render a clean report with `barista.table()`. This cell **depends on** the data cell above — run the pipeline, or *Run with deps*.",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "py601-2",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "python",
      "source": "//@ anchor: region-report\n//@ depends: sales-data\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\nby_region = defaultdict(lambda: {\"units\": 0, \"revenue\": 0})\nfor r in sales:\n    by_region[r[\"region\"]][\"units\"]   += r[\"units\"]\n    by_region[r[\"region\"]][\"revenue\"] += r[\"revenue\"]\n\nreport = [\n    {\"region\": reg, \"units\": v[\"units\"], \"revenue\": v[\"revenue\"],\n     \"avg_price\": round(v[\"revenue\"] / v[\"units\"], 2)}\n    for reg, v in sorted(by_region.items(), key=lambda kv: -kv[1][\"revenue\"])\n]\nbarista.table(report)",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0,
      "anchor": "region-report",
      "dependsOn": ["sales-data"]
    },
    {
      "id": "py601-md3",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "source": "## 3. Metric cards (KPI report) via HTML rendering\n\n`barista.html()` renders arbitrary HTML — perfect for a **KPI dashboard**. No charting library required.",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "py601-3",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "python",
      "source": "//@ depends: sales-data\ntotal_rev = sum(r[\"revenue\"] for r in sales)\ntotal_units = sum(r[\"units\"] for r in sales)\nbest = max(sales, key=lambda r: r[\"revenue\"])\navg_order = total_rev / len(sales)\n\ndef card(label, value, accent):\n    return (f'<div style=\"display:inline-block;min-width:150px;margin:6px;padding:14px 18px;'\n            f'border:1px solid #333;border-left:4px solid {accent};border-radius:10px;'\n            f'background:#1b1b24;font-family:sans-serif\">'\n            f'<div style=\"font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.05em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#9aa\">{label}</div>'\n            f'<div style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:700;color:#eee;margin-top:4px\">{value}</div></div>')\n\nbarista.html(\n    card(\"Total Revenue\", f\"${total_rev:,}\", \"#22c55e\") +\n    card(\"Units Sold\", f\"{total_units:,}\", \"#3b82f6\") +\n    card(\"Avg Order\", f\"${avg_order:,.0f}\", \"#f59e0b\") +\n    card(\"Top Line\", f'{best[\"region\"]} · {best[\"product\"]}', \"#a855f7\")\n)",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0,
      "dependsOn": ["sales-data"]
    },
    {
      "id": "py601-md4",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "source": "## 4. An inline SVG bar chart (zero dependencies)\n\nYou can render a real chart with nothing but Python + `barista.html()`, by emitting **SVG**. Great for quick visuals without installing anything.",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "py601-4",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "python",
      "source": "//@ depends: sales-data\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\nrev = defaultdict(int)\nfor r in sales:\n    rev[r[\"region\"]] += r[\"revenue\"]\n\nlabels = list(rev.keys())\nvalues = [rev[k] for k in labels]\nhi = max(values)\nW, bar_h, gap, left = 360, 26, 12, 60\nH = len(labels) * (bar_h + gap) + gap\n\nbars = []\nfor i, (lab, val) in enumerate(zip(labels, values)):\n    y = gap + i * (bar_h + gap)\n    w = int((val / hi) * (W - left - 70))\n    bars.append(\n        f'<text x=\"0\" y=\"{y+17}\" fill=\"#ccc\" font-size=\"12\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">{lab}</text>'\n        f'<rect x=\"{left}\" y=\"{y}\" width=\"{w}\" height=\"{bar_h}\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#3b82f6\"/>'\n        f'<text x=\"{left+w+6}\" y=\"{y+17}\" fill=\"#9aa\" font-size=\"11\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">${val:,}</text>')\n\nsvg = f'<svg width=\"{W}\" height=\"{H}\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">{\"\".join(bars)}</svg>'\nbarista.html('<div style=\"padding:8px 4px\"><b style=\"color:#eee;font-family:sans-serif\">Revenue by region</b></div>' + svg)",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0,
      "dependsOn": ["sales-data"]
    },
    {
      "id": "py601-md5",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "source": "## 5. NumPy — vectorized analytics 🧮\n\nFor real numeric work, **NumPy** is the foundation. Install `numpy` from **Packages → PyPI**, then run this. NumPy computes stats over whole arrays at C speed.",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "py601-5",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "python",
      "source": "try:\n    import numpy as np\nexcept ModuleNotFoundError:\n    print(\"numpy not installed — add it from Packages -> PyPI, then re-run.\")\nelse:\n    revenue = np.array([120, 80, 200, 60, 150, 110, 90, 130]) * np.array([30, 50, 30, 50, 30, 50, 30, 50])\n    print(\"mean:  \", revenue.mean())\n    print(\"median:\", np.median(revenue))\n    print(\"std:   \", round(float(revenue.std()), 2))\n    print(\"90th pct:\", np.percentile(revenue, 90))\n    # z-scores highlight outliers\n    z = (revenue - revenue.mean()) / revenue.std()\n    print(\"z-scores:\", np.round(z, 2))",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "py601-md6",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "source": "## 6. pandas — DataFrames + reporting 🐼\n\n**pandas** is the standard for tabular analysis. Install `pandas` from PyPI. We group, aggregate, and hand the result straight to `barista.table()` for rendering.",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "py601-6",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "python",
      "source": "//@ depends: sales-data\ntry:\n    import pandas as pd\nexcept ModuleNotFoundError:\n    print(\"pandas not installed — add it from Packages -> PyPI, then re-run.\")\nelse:\n    df = pd.DataFrame(sales)\n    pivot = (df.groupby(\"product\")\n               .agg(units=(\"units\", \"sum\"), revenue=(\"revenue\", \"sum\"))\n               .reset_index()\n               .sort_values(\"revenue\", ascending=False))\n    pivot[\"share_%\"] = (pivot[\"revenue\"] / pivot[\"revenue\"].sum() * 100).round(1)\n    barista.table(pivot.to_dict(\"records\"))",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0,
      "dependsOn": ["sales-data"]
    },
    {
      "id": "py601-md7",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "source": "## 7. matplotlib — publication charts 📊\n\nRender a **matplotlib** figure directly in the cell with `barista.image(fig)`. Install `matplotlib` from PyPI first.",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "py601-7",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "python",
      "source": "//@ depends: sales-data\ntry:\n    import matplotlib\n    matplotlib.use(\"Agg\")   # headless backend — render to image, no window\n    import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nexcept ModuleNotFoundError:\n    print(\"matplotlib not installed — add it from Packages -> PyPI, then re-run.\")\nelse:\n    from collections import defaultdict\n    rev = defaultdict(int)\n    for r in sales:\n        rev[r[\"region\"]] += r[\"revenue\"]\n    fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(6, 3.2))\n    ax.bar(list(rev.keys()), list(rev.values()), color=\"#3b82f6\")\n    ax.set_title(\"Revenue by region\"); ax.set_ylabel(\"USD\")\n    for i, v in enumerate(rev.values()):\n        ax.text(i, v + 80, f\"${v:,}\", ha=\"center\", fontsize=8)\n    barista.image(fig)\n    plt.close(fig)",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0,
      "dependsOn": ["sales-data"]
    },
    {
      "id": "py601-md8",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "source": "## You've completed the Python track! 🎉\n\nYou can now analyze data and build **rendered reports** — metric cards, tables, SVG and matplotlib charts — using Arima's default rendering, and scale up with the full PyPI ecosystem.\n\n**Recap of the built-in renderers:** `barista.table` · `barista.stats` · `barista.html` (cards, badges, SVG) · `barista.image` (matplotlib).\n\n> Combine cells into a **pipeline** (the `//@ anchor` / `//@ depends` lines above) to build a repeatable analysis + reporting workflow — and drive it from an agent or over MCP.",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    }
  ]
}
